      "text": "I would like to show you a couple of examples where this is a problem with medicine and AI. Our first one is just X-rays, X-ray case. And some peer-review studies show that state-of-the-art computer vision models under diagnosed underserved groups with higher risk miss rates from Hispanic women in chest X-ray tasks. A misfinding can delay, care, and worsening outcomes. Why this happens? And the representation in training, differences in image acquisition, or devices, label noise from automated report extraction, technical parameters in acquisition, and processing can shift fairness as well. What can we do about this? Benders must report so group false negative rates. Also, they should document acquisition factors and audit how pre-processing shift",
